ow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is true by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false. Again,  according to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." 13194139535482 17592186045443 true] [17592186323765 1071 "The system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x+2y+8z=0 \\\\ 2x-5y+4z=3 \\\\ 5x-4y+7z=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & a \\\\ 2 & b & 4 \\\\ 5 & c & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat are the values of $a,b$ and $c?$" 13194139535482 17592186045443 false] [17592186323765 1071 "The system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x+2y+8z=0 \\\\ 2x-5y+4z=3 \\\\ 5x-4y+7z=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & a \\\\ 2 & b & 4 \\\\ 5 & c & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat are the values of $a,b$ and $c?$" 13194139535482 17592186045443 true] [17592186323765 1074 "We can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=8,\\,b=-5,\\,c=-4.$" 13194139535482 17592186045443 false] [17592186323765 1074 "We can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=8,\\,b=-5,\\,c=-4.$" 13194139535482 17592186045443 true] [17592186323799 1074 "The coordinates of the vectors in $B$ relative to the basis $C$ are\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\,[{b}_{2}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535482 17592186045443 false] [17592186323799 1074 "The coordinates of the vectors in $B$ relative to the basis $C$ are\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\,[{b}_{2}]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139535482 17592186045443 true] [17592186323807 1071 "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given below. What is $[x]_{B}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535482 17592186045443 false] [17592186323807 1071 "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given below. What is $[x]_{B}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139535482 17592186045443 true] [17592186323807 1074 "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)\\cdot (-4)-3\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535482 17592186045443 false] [17592186323807 1074 "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)\\cdot (-4)-3\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begi